One sentence must not become two tasks

Found by the live call-to-PR run. The desk took longer than the 25-second
phone-side budget, so the handover filed a task of its own — while the router was
in the middle of filing the correctly-scoped one. Two tasks for one sentence, and
the handover's had no project, so the runner had no repo, so the harness wrote
outside its worktree and the isolation tripwire killed the run. One missing fact
at the top of that chain.

The missing fact was "the router has already filed this". `ingestUserMessage`
now takes an `onRouted` callback, fired the moment routing is done and before
the Supervisor is asked anything, so the phone runtime knows what exists without
waiting for the whole call. At the budget, if the router already filed it, the
handover files nothing and says the desk is still working — which is true, and a
second task for the same sentence is not.

A callback rather than a second entry point: the one-input-path rule is what
makes the phone and WhatsApp provably identical, and a parallel copy of that
function is how that stops being true.

Test — scripts/s22-desk-test.sh, now 43 assertions, 0 failed. The new section
sends a sentence carrying BOTH a work segment and a question, with the desk
forced slower than the budget — the shape the real call had, because a pure work
segment never reaches the Supervisor and so nothing would be slow:
- it handed over;
- one sentence, exactly one task;
- and it is the router's, with the project on it;
- the turn is recorded as handed over, pointing at the task that already exists.

Seen red first: removing the "already filed" check turned "exactly one task" and
"pointing at the task that already exists" red.

Also fixed in the live script: it looked for the task by PROJECT, so when the
handover filed a project-less one it reported "no task" while a task plainly
existed. It asks by inbox event now, and waits for the router rather than
declaring it missing. And the seed push goes out under the deploy key — the
first run died on "Host key verification failed" because it used whatever ssh
the operator had, which on that box is nothing.
